1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,707
This is MATH 239, video 6. The topic is the Binomial Theorem and the binomial series, section 2.1 of the notes.

2
00:00:10,157 --> 00:00:32,066
After this video, you can prove two formulas: one for 1 plus x to the n, and one for 1 over 1 minus x to the t. You can use them to find a coefficient of a product. We solve Exercises 2.1 and 2.2 of the notes, Tutorial 2 question 2, and Practice Midterm question 7.

3
00:00:32,516 --> 00:00:56,921
Chapter 2 calculates with power series. A power series G of x is a sum of g n times x to the n. Think of it as a row of coefficients. The coefficient of x to the n in G of x is written with square brackets: x to the n, then G of x. x is an indeterminate. It has no value. The first example: every coefficient is 1.

4
00:00:57,371 --> 00:01:07,526
Multiply G by x. Every term moves up one power. So each coefficient moves one cell to the right, and the first cell becomes 0.

5
00:01:07,976 --> 00:01:20,179
Subtract x G from G. In every cell after the first, 1 minus 1 is 0. Only the constant 1 stays. So 1 minus x, times G, equals 1.

6
00:01:20,629 --> 00:01:35,093
This is the geometric series. 1 over 1 minus x is the series 1 plus x plus x squared, and so on. We never put a number in for x, so we do not need convergence. This is algebra.

7
00:01:35,543 --> 00:01:54,081
Theorem 2.2 is the Binomial Theorem. For each natural number n, 1 plus x to the n equals the sum over k from 0 to n of n choose k, times x to the k. First, look at n equals 3: three factors of 1 plus x.

8
00:01:54,531 --> 00:02:05,219
To multiply out, take one term from each factor, and multiply. Here we take x from factor 1, and 1 from factors 2 and 3. The product is x.

9
00:02:05,669 --> 00:02:14,821
Each factor gives 2 choices. So there are 2 to the 3, which is 8, products. The orange cells show where each product takes x.

10
00:02:15,271 --> 00:02:24,466
Group the products by the power of x. One product is 1. Three products are x. Three are x squared. One is x cubed.

11
00:02:24,916 --> 00:02:45,140
So 1 plus x, all cubed, is 1 plus 3 x plus 3 x squared plus x cubed. The number of products equal to x to the k is the number of ways to pick the k factors that give x. That is n choose k. The notes prove this with a bijection. The next two slides show the proof.

12
00:02:45,590 --> 00:03:08,993
P of n is the set of all subsets of 1 to n. Curly 0 1 to the n is the set of indicator vectors: vectors of length n with entries 0 or 1. Example 1.12 gives a bijection. For n equals 8, the subset 2, 3, 5, 7 gives the vector with a 1 in positions 2, 3, 5 and 7.

13
00:03:09,443 --> 00:03:23,181
The inverse map collects the positions of the 1s. The two maps are mutually inverse bijections. Also, the entries of alpha add up to the size of S. Here 4 ones, and S has 4 elements.

14
00:03:23,631 --> 00:03:41,871
Put x to the size of S on each subset. Because of the bijection, the sum of x to the size of S over all subsets equals the sum of x to the a 1 plus up to a n, over all indicator vectors. The table shows all 8 pairs for n equals 3.

15
00:03:42,321 --> 00:03:54,503
The last column adds to 1 plus 3 x plus 3 x squared plus x cubed. This is 1 plus x, all cubed, again. Now we simplify the two sums in general.

16
00:03:54,953 --> 00:04:05,427
Start from the equation of the last slide. The left side, LHS, sums over subsets. The right side, RHS, sums over indicator vectors.

17
00:04:05,877 --> 00:04:21,365
Left side. Group the subsets by their size k. By Theorem 1.5, there are n choose k subsets of size k. Each gives x to the k. So the left side is the sum of n choose k, x to the k.

18
00:04:21,815 --> 00:04:40,333
Right side. A sum over all vectors is a sum over a 1 in 0, 1, then a 2 in 0, 1, and so on. Also x to the a 1 plus a 2 is x to the a 1 times x to the a 2. So the nested sums split into a product of n separate sums.

19
00:04:40,783 --> 00:04:56,548
Here is why the split is correct, for n equals 2. Each cell of the grid is one vector a 1, a 2. Its entry is x to the a 1 times x to the a 2. The sum of all cells is the row sum times the column sum.

20
00:04:56,998 --> 00:05:09,734
Each separate sum is x to the 0 plus x to the 1, which is 1 plus x. There are n of them, so the right side is 1 plus x to the n. This proves the Binomial Theorem.

21
00:05:10,184 --> 00:05:34,760
Example 2.3 uses the Binomial Theorem to prove the Vandermonde Convolution. Start with an obvious identity: 1 plus x to the m plus n equals 1 plus x to the m, times 1 plus x to the n. The grid shows m equals 3 and n equals 4. Each row is a term of the first factor. Each column is a term of the second.

22
00:05:35,210 --> 00:05:52,575
A cell is the product of its row term and its column term. The cell in row j and column i is m choose j, times n choose i, times x to the j plus i. All cells with the same j plus i are on one diagonal.

23
00:05:53,025 --> 00:06:07,340
The coefficient of x cubed on the right is the sum of the purple diagonal: 4 plus 18 plus 12 plus 1, which is 35. On the left, it is 7 choose 3, which is also 35.

24
00:06:07,790 --> 00:06:27,203
In general, re-index the double sum by k equals j plus i. Two equal polynomials have equal coefficients. So m plus n choose k equals the sum over j of m choose j times n choose k minus j. This is the Vandermonde Convolution.

25
00:06:27,653 --> 00:06:38,533
Now a series with infinitely many terms. For t equals 1, 1 over 1 minus x is the geometric series. Every coefficient is 1.

26
00:06:38,983 --> 00:07:06,631
Recall multisets from Chapter 1. M of t is the set of all multisets with t types of elements. A multiset is a multiplicity vector m 1 to m t of natural numbers. Its size is m 1 plus up to m t. By Theorem 1.9, the number of size n is n plus t minus 1, choose t minus 1. For t equals 2 this is n plus 1: 1, 2, 3, 4, and so on.

27
00:07:07,081 --> 00:07:18,601
For t equals 3, the counts are 1, 3, 6, 10, 15. The orange cell: there are 6 choose 2, which is 15, multisets of size 4 with 3 types.

28
00:07:19,051 --> 00:07:43,009
Theorem 2.4 is the binomial series. For each positive integer t, 1 over 1 minus x to the t equals the sum over n of n plus t minus 1, choose t minus 1, times x to the n. So the row for t is the multiset count for t types. The row for t equals 4 starts 1, 4, 10, 20.

29
00:07:43,459 --> 00:08:01,357
The notes call this the binomial series with a negative integer exponent. The general binomial series comes in Section 4.4.1. Also, n plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1 equals n plus t minus 1 choose n.

30
00:08:01,807 --> 00:08:20,815
The proof follows the same plan as the Binomial Theorem. A multiset with t types is a vector of t natural numbers. So M of t equals N to the t. By Theorem 1.9, the left sum collects x to the size of mu, over all multisets mu.

31
00:08:21,265 --> 00:08:39,804
A sum over N to the t is t nested sums, one for each m i, from 0 to infinity. And x to the size of mu is the product of x to the m i. The grid shows t equals 2. Cell m 1, m 2 holds x to the m 1 plus m 2.

32
00:08:40,254 --> 00:08:53,694
The purple cells have m 1 plus m 2 equal to 4. There are 5 of them. So the coefficient of x to the 4 is 5, which is 4 plus 1 choose 1. This matches the formula.

33
00:08:54,144 --> 00:09:09,376
The nested sums split into a product of t equal sums. Each one is the geometric series, 1 over 1 minus x. So the result is 1 over 1 minus x to the t. This proves Theorem 2.4.

34
00:09:09,826 --> 00:09:22,626
Exam questions use these formulas with a small change. Here is 1 over 1 minus y, the geometric series in a new indeterminate y. Every coefficient is 1.

35
00:09:23,076 --> 00:09:40,911
Now replace y by 3 x squared. The term y to the i becomes 3 to the i, times x to the 2 i. So the cell of y to the i moves to x to the 2 i, and gets the factor 3 to the i. The odd powers of x get 0.

36
00:09:41,361 --> 00:10:06,150
The general rule. Replace x by c x to the m in Theorem 2.4. The coefficient of x to the m i is i plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1, times c to the i. Other powers have coefficient 0. In the Binomial Theorem, replace x by c x: the coefficient of x to the k is n choose k times c to the k.

37
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:21,299
The shift rule. Multiplying by x to the a moves every cell a places to the right. So the coefficient of x to the n in x to the a times F equals the coefficient of x to the n minus a in F.

38
00:10:21,749 --> 00:10:35,722
Now some exercises from the notes. Exercise 2.1 asks for coefficients. Part a: the coefficient of x to the 8 in 1 minus x to the minus 7. Pause the video and try it.

39
00:10:36,172 --> 00:10:51,340
This is Theorem 2.4 with t equals 7. The coefficient of x to the n is n plus 6 choose 6. With n equals 8, we get 14 choose 6, which is 3003. The green cell shows it.

40
00:10:51,790 --> 00:11:01,369
Part b: the coefficient of x to the 10 in x to the 6, times 1 minus 2 x to the minus 5. Pause the video and try it.

41
00:11:01,819 --> 00:11:22,171
First expand 1 minus 2 x to the minus 5. Replace x by 2 x in Theorem 2.4 with t equals 5. The coefficient of x to the i is i plus 4 choose 4, times 2 to the i: 1, 10, 60, 280, 1120.

42
00:11:22,621 --> 00:11:40,541
Multiplying by x to the 6 moves the row 6 places to the right. So we need the coefficient of x to the 4 in the original row. That is 8 choose 4 times 2 to the 4, which is 70 times 16, or 1120.

43
00:11:40,991 --> 00:11:51,636
Part c: the coefficient of x to the 8 in x cubed plus 5 x to the 4, times 1 plus 3 x to the 6. Pause the video and try it.

44
00:11:52,086 --> 00:12:14,998
Split the product into two terms. In x cubed times F, the row of F moves 3 places. In 5 x to the 4 times F, it moves 4 places and every cell is multiplied by 5. By the shift rule, we need the coefficient of x to the 5 in F, plus 5 times the coefficient of x to the 4 in F.

45
00:12:15,448 --> 00:12:36,141
From the Binomial Theorem with x replaced by 3 x: 6 choose 5 times 3 to the 5 is 1458. 5 times 6 choose 4 times 3 to the 4 is 6075. The sum of the orange column is 7533.

46
00:12:36,591 --> 00:12:47,215
Part d: the coefficient of x to the 9 in 1 minus 4 x to the 5, plus 1 minus 3 x to the minus 2. Pause the video and try it.

47
00:12:47,665 --> 00:13:05,500
The coefficient of a sum is the sum of the coefficients. Look at the first term. 1 minus 4 x to the 5 has a positive exponent. By the Binomial Theorem, it is a polynomial. Its coefficients are 5 choose k times minus 4 to the k.

48
00:13:05,950 --> 00:13:17,406
A polynomial of degree 5 has no x to the 9 term. So the first part gives 0. Do not use the binomial series formula for a positive exponent.

49
00:13:17,856 --> 00:13:37,376
The second term is Theorem 2.4 with t equals 2 and x replaced by 3 x. The coefficient is 9 plus 1 choose 1, times 3 to the 9. That is 10 times 3 to the 9, which is 196,830. This is the answer.

50
00:13:37,826 --> 00:13:51,330
Tutorial 2, question 2. Find the coefficient of x to the 11 in x squared over 1 minus x cubed to the 5, times 1 minus 3 x squared. Pause the video and try it.

51
00:13:51,780 --> 00:14:02,575
Step 1, the shift. The factor x squared moves everything 2 places. So we need the coefficient of x to the 9 in the product of the two other factors.

52
00:14:03,025 --> 00:14:18,043
Step 2, expand each factor with Theorem 2.4. The first factor has terms i plus 4 choose 4, times x to the 3 i. The second has terms 3 to the j, times x to the 2 j.

53
00:14:18,493 --> 00:14:34,515
Step 3. A product of one term from each factor has exponent 3 i plus 2 j. The grid shows this exponent for each pair i, j. The rows are the terms of the first factor. The columns are the terms of the second.

54
00:14:34,965 --> 00:14:53,162
We need exponent 9. Two cells are green: i equals 1 with j equals 3, and i equals 3 with j equals 0. The red cells are larger than 9. Larger i or j only makes the exponent larger, so no other pair works.

55
00:14:53,612 --> 00:15:12,087
Step 4, add the products. i equals 1, j equals 3 gives 5 choose 4, times 3 cubed, which is 135. i equals 3, j equals 0 gives 7 choose 4, which is 35. The coefficient is 170.

56
00:15:12,537 --> 00:15:28,942
Practice Midterm question 7. Find the exact value of the coefficient of x to the 70 in x cubed, over 1 minus 4 x to the 30, to the 20, times 1 minus x, all cubed. Pause the video and try it.

57
00:15:29,392 --> 00:15:36,091
Step 1, the shift. Remove x cubed. Now we need the coefficient of x to the 67.

58
00:15:36,541 --> 00:16:05,703
Step 2, expand. The first factor is Theorem 2.4 with t equals 20, and x replaced by 4 x to the 30. Its terms are i plus 19 choose 19, times 4 to the i, times x to the 30 i. The second factor has t equals 3. Its terms are j plus 2 choose 2, times x to the j. We need 30 i plus j equal to 67. For i equals 0, j is 67.

59
00:16:06,153 --> 00:16:09,140
For i equals 1, j is 37.

60
00:16:09,590 --> 00:16:12,001
For i equals 2, j is 7.

61
00:16:12,451 --> 00:16:48,711
For i equals 3, 30 i is 90, which is more than 67. So there are three terms. Add them: 69 choose 2, plus 20 times 4 times 39 choose 2, plus 21 choose 19 times 16 times 9 choose 2. This is 2346 plus 59,280 plus 120,960, which is 182,586. The question allows you to stop at the sum of binomials.

62
00:16:49,161 --> 00:17:06,185
Exercise 2.2 goes the other way. Part f gives the first coefficients: 1, 6, 24, 80, 240, 672. Find a binomial series that begins like this. Pause the video and try it.

63
00:17:06,635 --> 00:17:25,323
From the substitution rule, a coefficient has the form c to the n times a binomial coefficient. The coefficients grow by about 2 each time, so try c equals 2. 80 is 8 times 10, and 672 is 32 times 21.

64
00:17:25,773 --> 00:17:44,248
Divide each coefficient by 2 to the n. The result is 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21. This is the row for t equals 3 on the binomial series slide: n plus 2 choose 2. So the series is 1 over 1 minus 2 x, cubed.

65
00:17:44,698 --> 00:17:54,106
Part e: 1 minus 4 x squared plus 12 x to the 4 minus 32 x to the 6, and so on. Pause the video and try it.

66
00:17:54,556 --> 00:18:10,364
Only even powers appear, so the series has x squared in place of x: m equals 2. Column n holds the coefficient of x to the 2 n. The signs alternate, so c is negative. Try c equals minus 2.

67
00:18:10,814 --> 00:18:30,974
Divide by minus 2 to the n. The result is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6. This is n plus 1 choose 1, the row for t equals 2. So the series is 1 over 1 minus minus 2 x squared, squared. That is 1 over 1 plus 2 x squared, all squared.

68
00:18:31,424 --> 00:18:40,469
Here is the method for every question of this type. Step 1: pull out any power x to the a, and lower the target exponent by a.

69
00:18:40,919 --> 00:18:49,922
Step 2: expand each factor with the Binomial Theorem or the binomial series. Write the general term with its own index.

70
00:18:50,372 --> 00:19:03,983
Step 3: write the exponent equation, for example 3 i plus 2 j equals 9. List every solution in natural numbers. Go through the values of the index with the largest step.

71
00:19:04,433 --> 00:19:09,617
Step 4: each solution gives one product of coefficients. Add the products.

72
00:19:10,067 --> 00:19:31,315
Common errors: a positive exponent gives a polynomial, so its coefficients are zero after x to the n. After the substitution of c x to the m, do not forget the factor c to the i. Do not miss a solution. In a written answer, name the result you use, show the solutions, and show the sum.

73
00:19:31,765 --> 00:19:44,266
To recap. The geometric series comes from 1 minus x, times G, equals 1. The Binomial Theorem comes from the bijection between subsets and indicator vectors.

74
00:19:44,716 --> 00:19:59,329
The Vandermonde Convolution compares coefficients in a product of two binomials. The binomial series for 1 over 1 minus x to the t comes from multisets, because M of t equals N to the t.

75
00:19:59,779 --> 00:20:15,801
To find a coefficient: substitute c x to the m, shift by x to the a, solve the exponent equation, and add the products. The next video makes formal power series exact: sums, products, inverses and composition.
